# ===== BOOKS BATCH 4 (38) =====
print("\n--- Books Batch 4 ---")
b4 = [
mk("BOOKS","Book","Crime and Punishment (1866)","Dostoevsky's psychological thriller about Raskolnikov, a destitute student who murders a pawnbroker. The novel was published in installments in a magazine and readers mobbed the publisher's office for each chapter. Dostoevsky wrote it while gambling away his advance, and the urgency is on every page. Raskolnikov's guilt is not about the murder but about being ordinary.","Fyodor Dostoevsky","Crime and Punishment, Part One",48,"Read Part One. Raskolnikov tests his theory: some men (like Napoleon) can transgress moral law. The murder scene is a masterpiece of tension — the doorbell rings, and everything unravels. Dostoevsky makes St. Petersburg a character: yellow walls, stifling rooms, crowds that judge silently. Notice Raskolnikov's fever dreams — they're the novel's moral compass.",["Russian","Fiction","Psychological","19th Century"],"crime-and-punishment"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Giovanni's Room (1956)","James Baldwin's novel about an American man in Paris torn between his girlfriend and an Italian bartender named Giovanni. Baldwin's publisher told him to burn the manuscript — it would ruin his career as a 'Negro' writer. He published anyway. The novel's narrator, David, is white, which was deliberate: Baldwin didn't want the novel dismissed as merely a 'Black' or 'gay' story.","James Baldwin","Giovanni's Room, the whole novel",42,"Read the whole novel — it's under 200 pages. David waits in the south of France for a train. Giovanni is about to be executed. Baldwin writes desire and self-loathing in the same sentence: David loves Giovanni and hates himself for it. The room of the title is where Giovanni hides David from the world — it becomes a tomb. Notice how Baldwin never lets David off the hook. The ending is a mirror.",["American","Fiction","Literary","20th Century"],"giovannis-room"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Normal People (2018)","Sally Rooney's novel about Marianne and Connell — he's the popular son of a cleaner, she's the weird rich girl. They pretend not to know each other at school, then meet again at Trinity College. The novel is about class, intimacy, and the words they can't say. The BBC adaptation broke viewing records.","Sally Rooney","Normal People, chapters 1-5",35,"Read the first five chapters. Connell's mother cleans Marianne's house. At school, they don't speak. Rooney writes without quotation marks, letting dialogue bleed into narration. The sex scenes are explicit and tender — they're where the characters are most honest. Notice the power shifts: in high school Marianne is beneath Connell; at Trinity the roles reverse. Class is not just money; it's who gets to speak and who has to listen.",["Irish","Fiction","Contemporary","21st Century"],"normal-people"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Fire Next Time (1963)","James Baldwin's book-length essay in the form of two letters — one to his 14-year-old nephew, one from 'a region of my mind.' Published in The New Yorker, it became an instant classic. Baldwin writes that 'God gave Noah the rainbow sign, No more water, the fire next time!' — the title is a warning. The essay is a diagnosis of American racism from the inside, written with love and fury.","James Baldwin","The Fire Next Time, the whole book",30,"Read the whole book — it's short but every sentence is loaded. Baldwin's letter to his nephew: 'You were born where you were born and faced the future that you faced because you were black and for no other reason.' The second essay is about his experience with the Nation of Islam and his realization that Christianity and Islam are both failing Black America. Notice the balance of tenderness and rage — Baldwin never sacrifices one for the other.",["American","Essays","Nonfiction","20th Century"],"the-fire-next-time"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Dubliners (1914)","James Joyce's collection of 15 short stories about Dublin in the early 20th century. Each story is a portrait of paralysis — the characters can't escape their city, their jobs, their memories. Joyce spent 10 years fighting publishers over the word 'bloody.' The collection ends with 'The Dead,' widely considered the greatest short story in English.","James Joyce","Dubliners, the first 7 stories",38,"Read the first seven stories. 'Araby' — a boy promises to bring a gift from a bazaar to a girl he barely knows. When he arrives as the stalls are closing, his romantic fantasy collapses. Joyce called these stories 'epiphanies' — moments when a character suddenly understands something about their life. Notice how little happens: the drama is interior. The paralysis is not just Dublin's; it's the human condition.",["Irish","Fiction","Short Stories","20th Century"],"dubliners"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Passion According to G.H. (1964)","Clarice Lispector's novel about a wealthy woman in Rio de Janeiro who enters her maid's room and crushes a cockroach — and from that act, has a mystical crisis that dissolves her entire sense of self. The novel is less a story than an extended meditation. Lispector described it as 'the best book I wrote, and I don't understand it.'","Clarice Lispector","The Passion According to G.H., the whole novel",40,"Read the whole novel — it's short but intense. G.H. (she is never fully named) enters the room that her maid has just left. Everything is clean, white, and terrifyingly empty. The cockroach is ancient, pre-human. When its white paste oozes out, G.H. eats it — and the world dissolves. Lispector's prose is a sustained panic attack: the sentences fragment, circle back, ask questions they can't answer. The novel is about the horror of being alive.",["Brazilian","Fiction","Philosophical","20th Century"],"the-passion-according-to-gh"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Season of Migration to the North (1966)","Tayeb Salih's novel about a Sudanese man who returns from studying in England to find a stranger, Mustafa Sa'eed, has settled in his village. Mustafa Sa'eed's story is Conrad's 'Heart of Darkness' reversed — the 'savage' goes to Europe and conquers it through seduction. The Arab Literary Academy named it the greatest Arabic novel of the 20th century.","Tayeb Salih","Season of Migration to the North, chapters 1-5",40,"Read the first five chapters. The narrator discovers Mustafa Sa'eed, a mysterious older man who quotes English poetry in a Sudanese village. Sa'eed's story emerges: a brilliant student sent to England who seduced English women as revenge for colonialism and was tried for murder of a woman he drove to suicide. Salih's prose is hypnotic — the Nile flows through every page. The novel asks: can the colonized ever go home?",["Sudanese","Fiction","Postcolonial","20th Century"],"season-of-migration-to-the-north"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","A Visit from the Goon Squad (2010)","Jennifer Egan's novel told in 13 interlinked stories, including one chapter written entirely as a PowerPoint presentation. It spans from the 1970s punk scene to a near-future New York, following Bennie Salazar, a record executive, and Sasha, his kleptomaniac assistant. Won the Pulitzer. Time is the 'goon squad' — it takes everything eventually.","Jennifer Egan","A Visit from the Goon Squad, chapters 1-4",38,"Read the first four chapters. Sasha steals a wallet on a date. Bennie sprays gold flakes in his coffee and can't feel music anymore. Each chapter shifts perspective and time, but characters recur in the background. The PowerPoint chapter (Chapter 12) is not a gimmick — each slide is a short story. Egan writes the music industry as a metaphor for aging: everyone's trying to stay relevant as time closes in.",["American","Fiction","Literary","21st Century"],"a-visit-from-the-goon-squad"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Pachinko (2017)","Min Jin Lee's multi-generational novel about a Korean family in Japan from 1910 to the 1980s. Koreans in Japan — Zainichi — were treated as permanent foreigners even after generations. The novel was rejected by every major publisher before becoming a National Book Award finalist. The title refers to pachinko parlors — one of the few businesses Zainichi Koreans could own.","Min Jin Lee","Pachinko, Book I",50,"Read Book I. Sunja, a teenager in a Korean fishing village, becomes pregnant by a married yakuza. A Christian minister offers to marry her and take her to Japan. Lee embeds the history of Japanese colonization of Korea in the smallest domestic moments: a bowl of rice, a boarding house, the registry that marks Koreans as aliens. The novel spans three generations; each one carries the weight of the previous.",["Korean-American","Historical Fiction","Literary","21st Century"],"pachinko-novel"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Canterbury Tales (c. 1400)","Geoffrey Chaucer's collection of 24 stories told by pilgrims traveling to Canterbury Cathedral. Each pilgrim tells a tale — knight, miller, wife of Bath, pardoner — in their own voice. Chaucer wrote in Middle English when that was revolutionary: French and Latin were the languages of literature. He died before finishing it; the tales we have are a fragment.","Geoffrey Chaucer","The Canterbury Tales, the General Prologue + 3 tales",48,"Read the General Prologue and three tales: The Knight's Tale, The Miller's Tale, and The Wife of Bath's Tale. Each pilgrim is introduced in a portrait of startling vividness — the Prioress who feeds her dogs better than the poor, the Friar who's more pimp than priest. The Miller's Tale is a fabliau about a cuckolded carpenter — it's filthy and hilarious. Notice the Wife of Bath: she's been married five times and has the best argument for female sovereignty in medieval literature.",["British","Poetry","Classic","Medieval"],"the-canterbury-tales"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Sympathizer (2015)","Viet Thanh Nguyen's Pulitzer-winning novel, already described above.","Viet Thanh Nguyen","The Sympathizer, full novel","","",[],"sympathizer-dupe",0),  # Placeholder, never processed
mk("BOOKS","Book","Austerlitz (2001)","W.G. Sebald's final novel about a man who arrives in England on a Kindertransport at age 4 and doesn't learn his real name or origin until he's retired. The novel is narrated as a series of conversations, accompanied by uncaptioned photographs — they feel like evidence but prove nothing. Sebald died in a car crash weeks after finishing it.","W.G. Sebald","Austerlitz, the whole novel",50,"Read the whole novel — it's one long paragraph punctuated by photographs. Jacques Austerlitz, an architectural historian, tells his story to the narrator in train stations and cafes across Europe. He was a child refugee from Prague; his mother died in Theresienstadt. Sebald's sentences are immense — they hold decades in one breath. The photographs are uncaptioned: a building, a boy in a costume, a pair of eyes. They haunt the text without explaining it.",["German","Fiction","Historical","21st Century"],"austerlitz"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","A Wizard of Earthsea (1968)","Ursula K. Le Guin's fantasy novel about a boy named Ged who goes to a wizard school and releases a shadow monster in an act of pride. He spends the rest of the novel being hunted by his own darkness. Le Guin wrote it as a response to the lack of non-white heroes in fantasy — all the characters in Earthsea are brown-skinned. It was published decades before Harry Potter and says more about the danger of magical power in 200 pages than Rowling does in 7 books.","Ursula K. Le Guin","A Wizard of Earthsea, the whole novel",42,"Read the whole novel. Ged, a goatherd with a gift for magic, is trained at the school of Roke. In a duel of pride, he summons a shadow from the land of the dead. The shadow pursues him; eventually he realizes he must stop running and turn to face it. The novel is structured like a Taoist parable: the shadow is Ged's own death, and naming it is the only way to be free. Le Guin's magic has moral weight — every spell has consequences.",["American","Fantasy","Young Adult","20th Century"],"a-wizard-of-earthsea"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Bluest Eye (1970)","Toni Morrison's debut novel about Pecola Breedlove, a Black girl who prays for blue eyes, believing they will make her beautiful and loved. The novel is told in fragments — from multiple perspectives, across years — because Pecola's trauma can't be narrated in a straight line. Morrison wrote it while working as an editor at Random House, getting up at 4 AM to write before her children woke.","Toni Morrison","The Bluest Eye, the whole novel",45,"Read the whole novel. The novel opens with a Dick-and-Jane primer that fractures into nonsense — the safe white world is breaking apart. Pecola's story is told by Claudia, a girl who survives what Pecola doesn't. Morrison shows racism as a poison that turns inward: Pecola believes if she had blue eyes, she would be beautiful. The novel's tragedy is not just what happens to Pecola but that her community participates in destroying her.",["American","Fiction","Literary","20th Century"],"the-bluest-eye"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Braiding Sweetgrass (2013)","Robin Wall Kimmerer's book about indigenous wisdom and botany. Kimmerer is a Potawatomi botanist and professor who bridges western science and Native knowledge. Each chapter weaves together ecology, storytelling, and memoir. The title comes from the Potawatomi story of Skywoman, who brought plants to earth. The book was a slow-burn bestseller that became a phenomenon years after publication.","Robin Wall Kimmerer","Braiding Sweetgrass, Part One: Planting Sweetgrass",42,"Read Part One. Kimmerer begins with Skywoman falling from the sky, creating Turtle Island with a handful of mud. Then she describes pecans, strawberries, and the grammar of animacy in Potawatomi — English treats the natural world as 'it'; Potawatomi treats it as someone. Notice how Kimmerer refuses to choose between science and spirituality: they are two languages for the same love. 'In some Native languages the term for plants translates to those who take care of us.'",["Native American","Nonfiction","Nature","21st Century"],"braiding-sweetgrass"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","On Earth We're Briefly Gorgeous (2019)","Ocean Vuong's debut novel written as a letter from a Vietnamese-American son to his illiterate mother. Vuong is a poet, and the novel reads like a long poem — every sentence is chiseled. It moves between the Vietnam War, a nail salon in Hartford, and a first love with a white boy in tobacco fields. The novel is autobiographical in spirit but Vuong insists it's fiction.","Ocean Vuong","On Earth We're Briefly Gorgeous, Part One",38,"Read Part One. 'Let me begin again.' Little Dog writes to his mother Rose, who cannot read. He describes his grandmother Lan, who escaped an arranged marriage during the war, and his first love, Trevor, who taught him to drive a combine harvester. Vuong writes about the body — his mother's hands scarred from the nail salon, Trevor's body in the tobacco fields — as the site where love and violence are the same thing. The monarch butterfly is the novel's recurring image: migration, transformation, fragile beauty.",["Vietnamese-American","Fiction","Literary","21st Century"],"on-earth-were-briefly-gorgeous"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","If Beale Street Could Talk (1974)","James Baldwin's novel about a young Black couple in 1970s Harlem — Tish and Fonny. Fonny is in prison for a rape he didn't commit, and Tish is pregnant with his child. The novel is narrated by Tish, whose voice is steady and fierce. Baldwin wrote it as a love story, not a protest novel — but love under racism is protest. Adapted into an Oscar-winning film in 2018.","James Baldwin","If Beale Street Could Talk, the whole novel",40,"Read the whole novel. Tish tells Fonny — and us — how they fell in love. She's 19 and pregnant; her family rallies around Fonny while his own family fractures. Baldwin writes love as the only resistance that matters: Tish's family pools their money for lawyers, brings food, holds each other. The novel is about the carceral system but never lets the system overshadow the people. Tish's narration is the novel's miracle — she's never bitter, only certain.",["American","Fiction","Romance","20th Century"],"if-beale-street-could-talk"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Vaster Wilds (2023)","Lauren Groff's novel about a servant girl who escapes a 17th-century colonial settlement in Virginia during a famine and runs into the wilderness with nothing but her wits. The novel is a survival story and a spiritual crisis told in prose that feels like scripture. Groff wrote it in a fever during the pandemic.","Lauren Groff","The Vaster Wilds, Part One",38,"Read Part One. A girl known only as 'the girl' or 'the servant' flees the Jamestown settlement in the dead of winter. She has no name because she was never given one that mattered. The wilderness is violent — wolves, starvation, exposure — but also holy. Groff's prose is stripped to its essentials: every sentence is physical. The girl's spiritual crisis intensifies as she starves: she sees God in a flash of light on ice and questions whether God has ever seen her.",["American","Historical Fiction","Literary","21st Century"],"the-vaster-wilds"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Alchemist (1988)","Paulo Coelho's allegorical novel about Santiago, a Spanish shepherd who dreams of treasure at the pyramids. The novel was a slow seller at first — Coelho's first publisher dropped him — but has now sold over 65 million copies in 80+ languages. It's one of the best-selling books in history and the most translated book by a living author.","Paulo Coelho","The Alchemist, Part One",25,"Read Part One. Santiago sells his sheep and sails to Tangier. He's robbed, works for a crystal merchant, and joins a caravan crossing the Sahara. Coelho writes in simple declarative sentences — the prose feels like folklore. 'When you want something, all the universe conspires in helping you to achieve it.' The novel is less a plot than a parable: the treasure was never at the pyramids. Notice the alchemist himself appears only in the last third.",["Brazilian","Fiction","Allegorical","20th Century"],"the-alchemist"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Bee Sting (2023)","Paul Murray's novel about the Barnes family — an Irish family unraveling after the 2008 financial crash. The novel is structured in four parts, each from a different family member's perspective. It was shortlisted for the Booker Prize. The novel is funny, devastating, and 650 pages that feel like 300.","Paul Murray","The Bee Sting, Part One: Cass",42,"Read Part One. Cass Barnes, 17, is drinking in the woods with her friend Elaine when the novel opens. Her father's car dealership is failing; her mother is selling things on eBay; her brother is building a bunker. Murray writes Irish family life with comic precision and then turns the screw: the financial crash is a slow-motion car wreck that everyone sees coming and no one can stop. The bee sting of the title is both literal and metaphorical.",["Irish","Fiction","Contemporary","21st Century"],"the-bee-sting"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Midnight Library (2020)","Matt Haig's novel about Nora Seed, who tries to kill herself and finds herself in a library between life and death — each book contains a different version of her life she could have lived. The novel is about regret and the paths not taken. Haig wrote it after his own mental health crisis and it became a global bestseller.","Matt Haig","The Midnight Library, the whole novel",38,"Read the whole novel. Nora lands in the Midnight Library presided over by her old school librarian Mrs. Elm. She can try any life — Olympic swimmer, rock star, glaciologist, mother — and if she finds one worth living, she stays. The premise is simple but Haig executes it with empathy: each life teaches Nora something about the one she already has. The novel's philosophy is not 'anything is possible' but 'what you have is already enough.'",["British","Fiction","Speculative","21st Century"],"the-midnight-library"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Kindred (1979)","Octavia Butler's novel about Dana, a Black woman in 1976 Los Angeles who is suddenly transported to a Maryland plantation in 1815. She is pulled back in time whenever her white ancestor Rufus is in mortal danger — she must keep him alive long enough to father her ancestor. Butler called it 'grim fantasy' — there's no explanation for the time travel, just the unflinching reality of slavery.","Octavia Butler","Kindred, the whole novel",48,"Read the whole novel. Dana's first trip to the past: she saves a drowning white boy named Rufus and is nearly shot by his father. The time travel is involuntary and brutal — Dana arrives disoriented and returns with injuries. Butler doesn't romanticize the past: Dana must live as a slave, and the novel documents the daily violence without flinching. Rufus grows from a frightened boy into a slaveholder who believes he owns Dana. The horror is not the time travel; it's history.",["American","Science Fiction","Historical","20th Century"],"kindred"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Yellowface (2023)","R.F. Kuang's novel about June Hayward, a white writer who steals her dead Chinese-American friend's manuscript and publishes it as her own — complete with an ethnically ambiguous author photo. What follows is a satire of the publishing industry, cancel culture, and literary appropriation. The novel went viral on BookTok and became a bestseller.","R.F. Kuang","Yellowface, the whole novel",35,"Read the whole novel. Athena Liu dies choking on a pancake in front of June, who takes her unpublished manuscript about Chinese laborers in WWI. June edits it, changes her name to Juniper Song, and becomes a literary star. The novel works as thriller and satire: June's narration is a masterclass in self-justification. Kuang makes you understand June even as you recoil. The publishing industry — sensitivity readers, Twitter pile-ons, diversity panels — is skewered with insider knowledge.",["American","Fiction","Satire","21st Century"],"yellowface"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","The Sellout (2015)","Paul Beatty's novel about a Black man who reinstates slavery and segregation in his Los Angeles neighborhood — as satire — and ends up in the Supreme Court. Won the Booker Prize (the first American to win it). Every sentence is a joke that's serious. Beatty's narrator is an urban farmer who grows satsumas and watermelons and calls himself a 'sellout' for loving the neighborhood no one else cares about.","Paul Beatty","The Sellout, chapters 1-8",38,"Read the first eight chapters. The narrator (unnamed) is raised by a sociologist father who used him as a subject in racial psychology experiments. His father is shot by the police, and the narrator's response is absurdity: he takes a slave, paints a line of segregation on the street. Beatty's prose is hip-hop and literature at once — dense with references, profane, hilarious. The satire is so sharp it hurts: the novel is about how race is simultaneously everything and nothing.",["American","Fiction","Satire","21st Century"],"the-sellout"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Don Quixote (1605/1615)","Cervantes' novel about a man who reads so many chivalric romances that he decides to become a knight-errant. It's the first modern novel and invented metafiction: the characters in Part Two have read Part One. Nabokov called it 'the cruelest book ever written' because everyone laughs at Quixote's madness. Cervantes published Part One at 58 after a life of captivity, excommunication, and failure.","Miguel de Cervantes","Don Quixote, chapters 1-10",48,"Read the first ten chapters. Alonso Quijano renames himself Don Quixote, makes a helmet out of pasteboard, and rides out on a broken-down horse. His first battle: windmills he thinks are giants. Sancho Panza, a peasant, becomes his squire on the promise of an island. Cervantes writes Quixote's madness with tenderness — the knight sees what we wish the world contained. The innkeeper who knights him is humoring a fool, but the ceremony is real to Quixote.",["Spanish","Fiction","Classic","17th Century"],"don-quixote"),

mk("BOOKS","Book","Their Eyes Were Watching God (1937)","Zora Neale Hurston's novel about Janie Crawford's journey through three marriages to selfhood in rural Florida. Out of print for decades, rescued in 1975 by Alice Walker, who put a gravestone on Hurston's unmarked grave. The novel is written in lyrical third person and Black Southern dialect — the two registers are the novel's music.","Zora Neale Hurston","Their Eyes Were Watching God, chapters 1-6",40,"Read the first six chapters. Janie returns to Eatonville and tells her story. Her grandmother marries her off at 16 — she 'had been summoned to behold a revelation.' Janie's journey is toward the horizon: not a man but a self. Hurston's prose is incantatory: 'Ships at a distance have every man's wish on board.' The hurricane section is one of the most terrifying passages in American literature. Janie survives it all — and the novel ends with her pulling in her horizon 'like a great fish-net.'",["American","Fiction","Harlem Renaissance","20th Century"],"their-eyes-were-watching-god"),
]
